WEBVTT

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:07.970
Martian air is about 95% carbon dioxide, and far too thin to breathe.

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.930
Could we make our own oxygen there?

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:22.120
In 2021, a box the size of a car battery rode to Mars inside the Perseverance rover: MOXIE.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:33.030
Between 2021 and 2023 it ran 16 times and made 122 grams of oxygen, up to 12 grams an hour.

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:44.720
Inside, a compressor squeezes the thin air, and a ceramic cell heated to 800 degrees splits each carbon dioxide molecule.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:49.900
Oxygen goes one way. Carbon monoxide goes back to the air.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:01:00.210
A person breathes under a kilogram of oxygen a day. A rocket leaving Mars needs about 25 tonnes.

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:06.910
Now compare your plant. Every kilogram made there is a kilogram you don't carry from Earth.

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:13.700
<v Franchesca>We used to ask if Mars could hold life. Now we ask if it can help us live.

